---
import '@kyralabs/m3e-css/button.css';
import { springToLinear } from '@kyralabs/m3e-motion';
import { motion } from '@kyralabs/m3e-tokens';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ScrollTable from '../../components/ScrollTable.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { kebab } from '../../lib/kebab';

// The six springs of each motion scheme (StandardMotionTokens.kt / ExpressiveMotionTokens.kt,
// generated), sampled into the same linear() curves the CSS tokens carry (m3e-motion).
const SPEEDS = ['fast', 'default', 'slow'] as const;
const TYPES = ['Spatial', 'Effects'] as const;
type Scheme = keyof typeof motion.springs;
const SCHEMES: Scheme[] = ['expressive', 'standard'];
const springs = SCHEMES.flatMap((scheme) =>
  TYPES.flatMap((type) =>
    SPEEDS.map((speed) => {
      const key = `${speed}${type}` as keyof (typeof motion.springs)[Scheme];
      const spec = motion.springs[scheme][key];
      const curve = springToLinear(spec);
      return { scheme, key, token: kebab(key), spec, curve };
    }),
  ),
);

// Each curve as an SVG polyline: time 0–1 across, value up (−0.1 to 1.3, room for overshoot).
const W = 100;
const H = 60;
const toY = (v: number) => H - ((v + 0.1) / 1.4) * H;
const points = (stops: readonly (readonly [number, number])[]) =>
  stops.map(([t, v]) => `${(t * W).toFixed(2)},${toY(v).toFixed(2)}`).join(' ');
const rest = toY(1).toFixed(2);
---

<Guide
  title="Motion"
  description="Motion in M3 Expressive is physics: springs with a damping ratio and a stiffness, in two schemes. Spatial springs move things and may overshoot; effects springs fade and recolor, and never do."
  summary="Material 3 Expressive motion springs: damping and stiffness of the spatial and effects springs in both schemes, with curves and a live demo."
>
  <h2 id="springs">The springs</h2>
  <p>
    Each scheme has six springs: fast, default and slow, for spatial and for effects changes. Use
    <strong>spatial</strong> springs for position, size, scale, rotation, corner radius and shape
    morph; <strong>effects</strong> springs for color, opacity and elevation. Fast suits small
    components, default most transitions, slow large or full-screen ones. The expressive scheme is
    the default; <code>createTheme({'{'} motionScheme: 'standard' {'}'})</code> switches.
  </p>
  <ScrollTable label="Spring values">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Spring</th>
          <th scope="col">Expressive ζ / k</th>
          <th scope="col">Standard ζ / k</th>
          <th scope="col">Expressive settle (ms)</th>
          <th scope="col">Standard settle (ms)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          TYPES.flatMap((type) =>
            SPEEDS.map((speed) => {
              const key = `${speed}${type}`;
              const e = springs.find((s) => s.scheme === 'expressive' && s.key === key)!;
              const s = springs.find((x) => x.scheme === 'standard' && x.key === key)!;
              return (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                    <code>{e.token}</code>
                  </th>
                  <td>
                    {e.spec.dampingRatio} / {e.spec.stiffness}
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    {s.spec.dampingRatio} / {s.spec.stiffness}
                  </td>
                  <td>{e.curve.durationMillis}</td>
                  <td>{s.curve.durationMillis}</td>
                </tr>
              );
            }),
          )
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ScrollTable>
  <p>
    ζ is the damping ratio (1 never overshoots) and k the stiffness, with a mass of 1. The settle
    time is Compose's own estimate for the spring, the duration of its CSS curve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="curves">Curves</h2>
  <p>
    Progress over each spring's settle time; the dashed line is the end value. Expressive spatial
    springs overshoot and come back; effects springs approach it from below.
  </p>
  <ul class="site-spring-curves">
    {
      springs.map((s) => (
        <li class="site-spring-curves__item">
          <svg class="site-spring-curves__svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${String(W)} ${String(H)}`} aria-hidden="true">
            <line class="site-spring-curves__rest" x1="0" x2={W} y1={rest} y2={rest} />
            <polyline class="site-spring-curves__line" points={points(s.curve.stops)} />
          </svg>
          <span>
            {s.scheme} · {s.token}
          </span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="demo">Try them</h2>
  <p>Each dot crosses its track with one spring's CSS curve and duration. Press Play again to go back.</p>
  <div class="site-spring-demo" data-spring-demo>
    <button type="button" class="m3e-button m3e-button--filled m3e-button--size-s site-js-only" data-spring-play>
      <span class="m3e-icon" aria-hidden="true">play_arrow</span>
      Play
    </button>
    <ul class="site-spring-demo__tracks">
      {
        springs
          .filter((s) => s.key.endsWith('Spatial'))
          .map((s) => (
            <li class="site-spring-demo__track">
              <span class="site-spring-demo__label">
                {s.scheme} · {s.token}
              </span>
              <span
                class="site-spring-demo__dot"
                style={[
                  `transition-timing-function: var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-${s.scheme}-${s.token}-easing)`,
                  `transition-duration: var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-${s.scheme}-${s.token}-duration)`,
                ].join('; ')}
              />
            </li>
          ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="css">In CSS</h2>
  <p>
    Each spring is a <code>linear()</code> easing and a duration: Compose's spring simulation
    sampled every millisecond and reduced to at most 40 stops. The unprefixed tokens follow the
    active scheme; the <code>-standard-</code> and <code>-expressive-</code> ones are fixed. A
    cubic-bezier fallback from m3.material.io is there too (<code>-bezier</code>,
    <code>-bezier-duration</code>).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    label="spring CSS"
    code={`.panel {
  transition:
    translate var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-default-spatial-duration)
      var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-default-spatial-easing),
    opacity var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-default-effects-duration)
      var(--m3e-sys-motion-spring-default-effects-easing);
}`}
  />
  <p>
    A CSS curve cannot carry velocity into an interruption: it restarts. Gestures, drags and fast
    toggles use the JavaScript springs in <code>@kyralabs/m3e-motion</code> instead, as the
    components do. Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> the spatial tokens snap
    (duration 0) and the default and slow effects use the fast effects curve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Spring parameters: <code>StandardMotionTokens.kt</code> L20–31 and
      <code>ExpressiveMotionTokens.kt</code> L22–33 at the pinned commit, generated
      (FOUNDATIONS_SPEC §4).
    </li>
    <li>
      Curves: a port of Compose's <code>SpringSimulation</code>; durations from
      <code>FloatSpringSpec.getDurationNanos</code>. Bezier fallback: m3.material.io, Motion
      overview › Specs.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Guide>

<script>
  for (const demo of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-spring-demo]')) {
    demo.querySelector('[data-spring-play]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      demo.toggleAttribute('data-played');
    });
  }
</script>
